\end{figure}
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
- **Dialog boxes**: small windows that display information and allow the user to
  perform actions
  - Responsible for most of the interaction through a GUI
  - User interacts with graphical elements such as icons, buttons and sliders
::::
:::

# GUI Programs are Event-Driven

::: columns
:::: {.column width=66%}
- In text-based (command line driven) environments, programs determine the order in which
  things happen
  - The user can only enter data in the order requested by the program (e.g. sequence of prompts)
- GUI environments is **event-driven**
  - The user determines the order in which things happen
  - User causes events to take place and the program responds to the events
::::
:::: {.column width=33%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.33]{figures/ch13-fig-13-3-tkinter-gui-program}
\label{fig:ch13-3-tkinter-gui-program}
\caption{A basic Python tkinter GUI program \parencite[pg.653]{gaddis-2021}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::

# Using the Python `tkinter` Module (1 of 3)

- No GUI programming features built into Python
- **`tkinter` module**: allows you to create a simple GUI program
  - Comes with Python
- **Widget**: a graphical element that the user can interact with or view
  - Presented by a GUI program

# Using the Python `tkinter` Module (2 of 3)

\tiny
| **Widget**    | **Description**                                                                                                                                          |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `Button`      | A button that can cause an action to occur when it is clicked.                                                                                           |
| `Canvas`      | A rectangular area that can be used to display graphics.                                                                                                 |
| `Checkbutton` | A button that may be in either the "on" or "off" position.                                                                                               |
| `Entry`       | An area in which the user may type a single line of input from the keyboard.                                                                             |
| `Frame`       | A container that can hold other widgets.                                                                                                                 |
| `Label`       | An area that displays one line of text or an image.                                                                                                      |
| `Listbox`     | A list from which the user may select an item.                                                                                                           |
| `Menu`        | A list of menu choices that are displayed when the user clicks a `Menubutton` widget.                                                                    |
| `Menubutton`  | A menu that is displayed on the screen and may be clicked by the user.                                                                                   |
| `Message`     | Displays multiple lines of text.                                                                                                                         |
| `Radiobutton` | A widget that can be either selected or deselected.  `Radiobutton` widgets usually appear in groups and allow the user to select one of several options. |
| `Scale`       | A widget that allows the user to select a value by moving a slider along a track.                                                                        |
| `Scrollbar`   | Can be used with some other types of widgets to provide scrolling ability.                                                                               |
| `Text`        | A widget that allows the user to enter multiple lines of text input.                                                                                     |
| `Toplevel`    | A container, like a `Frame`, but displayed in its own window.                                                                                            |

Table: Some `tkinter` widgets

# Using the Python `tkinter` Module (3 of 3)

- Programs that use `tkinter` do not always run reliably under IDLE (or in containers).
  - For best results run them from the operating system command prompt
  - For containers, may need to ensure that external display of gui widgets (X windows config) is configured properly
- Most programmers take an object-oriented approach when writing GUI programs
  - `__init__` method builds the GUI
  - When an instance is created the GUI appears on the screen

# Display Text with `Label` Widgets

- **`Label` widget**: displays a single line of text in a window
  - Made by creating an instance of `tkinter` module's `Label` class
  - Format: `tkinter.Label(self.main_window, text='my text')`
  - First argument references the root widget, second argument shows text that should appear in label
- **`pack()` method**: determines where a widget should be positioned and makes it visible when the main window
  is displayed
  - Called for each widget in a window
  - Receives an argument to specify positioning
    - Positioning depends n the order in which widgets were added to the main window
    - Valid arguments: `side='top'`, `side='left'`, `side='right'`

# Adding Borders to Labels

::: columns
:::: {.column width=66%}
- When creating a `Label` widget, you can  use the `borderwidth` and `relief` arguments
  to display a border around the label
- The `borderwidth` argument specifies the width of the border in pixels
- The `relief` argument specifies the border style
::::
:::: {.column width=33%}
\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.5]{figures/ch13-fig-13-11-label-relief-styles}
\label{fig:ch13-11-label-relief-styles}
\caption{Examples of different styles of borders \parencite[pg.661]{gaddis-2021}}
\end{figure}
::::
:::

# Internal and External Padding (1 of 2)

- **Padding**: space that appears around a widget
  - **Internal padding** appears around the inside edge of a widget
  - **External padding** appears around the outside edge of a widget

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.7]{figures/ch13-fig-13-12-padding}
\label{fig:ch13-12-padding}
\caption{Internal and external padding \parencite[pg.662]{gaddis-2021}}
\end{figure}


# Internal and External Padding (2 of 2)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- To add horizontal internal padding to a widget, pass the argument `ipadx=n` to the widget's pack method
- To add vertical internal padding pass the argument `ipady=n` to the widget's pack method

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.30]{figures/ch13-fig-13-13-internal-padding}
\label{fig:ch13-13-internal-padding}
\caption{Horizontal and vertical internal padding \parencite[pg.662]{gaddis-2021}}
\end{figure}

::::
:::: {.column width=50%}
- To add horizontal external padding to a widget, pass the argument `padx=n` to the widget's pack method
- To add vertical external padding pass the argument `pady=n` to the widget's pack method

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.28]{figures/ch13-fig-13-15-external-padding}
\label{fig:ch13-15-external-padding}
\caption{Horizontal and vertical external padding \parencite[pg.664]{gaddis-2021}}
\end{figure}

::::
:::

# Summary

- The **user interface** is the part of the computer with which the user interacts
- A **graphical user interface** or **GUI** allows users to interact with a program through graphical elements
  on the screen
- GUIs are inherently **event-driven**, this means that the user determines the order and timing of when
  to interact with and cause things to happen
  - This requires an **event-loop** with callbacks to handle GUI interface interactions
- The `tkinter` module provides basic GUI elements and is available in the base Python packaging system
  - There is a more modern set of themed user interface components that is part of the base tkinter
    [See tkinter.ttk](https://docs.python.org/3/library/tkinter.ttk.html#module-tkinter.ttk) \parencite{tkinter-docs-2026}
  - But there are more modern looking extensions, see [customtkinter](https://customtkinter.tomschimansky.com/)
    \parencite{customtkinter-docs-2026} 
- Most programmers prefer to take an object-oriented approach when writing a GUI program
  - For Python `tkinter` this usually means creating GUI elements in `__init__` method
  - as well as usually starting the main event loop after GUI elements are created
- Use the `tkinter` `Label` widget to display text in a window
  - You arrange widgets by calling `pack()` with various positioning arguments
  - Order you add widgets to a container will effect layout (sometimes, e.g. see grids)


